WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.390
Foundations. Two kinds of work.

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.970
Who does what, and which path is mine?

3
00:00:13.512 --> 00:00:15.522
The work here comes in two kinds.

4
00:00:15.592 --> 00:00:17.832
The line is what carries a change:

5
00:00:17.832 --> 00:00:20.792
the template and its
workflows, the shared stack,

6
00:00:20.792 --> 00:00:22.642
and the automation that runs them.

7
00:00:23.352 --> 00:00:25.442
The product is what rides on the line:

8
00:00:25.512 --> 00:00:29.272
each service's code, its
Azure provider, its tests,

9
00:00:29.272 --> 00:00:32.002
and what the service promises its callers.

10
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.150
A lot of the work opens itself.

11
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.630
Automation opens the sync, cascade,
and release pull requests,

12
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.830
and the ones that keep dependencies
and the template in step.

13
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.870
Copilot may review a pull request first.

14
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.150
But nothing reaches main until
a code owner approves it,

15
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.510
after its last push, with
the required checks green.

16
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.550
And when the line can't go on alone, it
stops and opens an issue for a person.

17
00:01:00.816 --> 00:01:03.536
If you change what a
service does, its features,

18
00:01:03.536 --> 00:01:06.856
its provider, its tests,
that's service work,

19
00:01:06.856 --> 00:01:08.746
and the service path is yours.

20
00:01:08.976 --> 00:01:12.176
If you change what carries
every service, the stack,

21
00:01:12.176 --> 00:01:16.106
the template, the shared
environment, that's platform work,

22
00:01:16.296 --> 00:01:18.986
and the platform path is yours.

23
00:01:19.216 --> 00:01:22.026
One person can do both in the same week.

24
00:01:24.652 --> 00:01:26.542
Take one upstream change again.

25
00:01:26.772 --> 00:01:31.102
At midnight, sync opens its pull
request, and a person merges it.

26
00:01:31.252 --> 00:01:36.542
Cascade opens the next one, into main,
and it waits for a code owner's approval.

27
00:01:36.692 --> 00:01:40.702
The release pull request waits until
a person decides the version is ready.

28
00:01:40.932 --> 00:01:42.542
Automation did the work.

29
00:01:42.692 --> 00:01:44.382
A person made each call.

30
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.810
So now you can say:

31
00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.970
the line carries the product,
automation opens much of the work,

32
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.370
a person approves what reaches
main, and your work picks your path.

33
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.850
Next, take your path.

34
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.970
Service work starts with
your own environment.

35
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.250
Platform work starts with
how it comes to life.

